const textbookDiagrams = {"equipment-model.svg": {"title": "Equipment loan model in crow's-foot notation", "description": "A Category has zero or many Equipment items; each Equipment has exactly one Category. A Borrower has zero or many Loans; each Loan has exactly one Borrower. Equipment has zero or many Loans over time; each Loan references exactly one item."}, "social-preview.svg": {"title": "Computer Science ATAR: a free, open classroom textbook", "description": "Green and cream textbook artwork for WA Units 1–4. Free to use, share and adapt with attribution."}, "unit2-six-table-model.svg": {"title": "Six-table equipment model extension", "description": "Category and Supplier each have zero or many Equipment; Equipment and Borrower each have zero or many Loan; Equipment has zero or many Inspection. Every child has exactly one corresponding parent. This is a conceptual extension, not the starter database."}, "unit3-inheritance-model.svg": {"title": "Equipment inheritance hierarchy", "description": "Camera and Projector inherit from Equipment. Hollow triangle arrows point to the superclass. Stock operations are inherited and preparation is overridden."}, "unit3-layer-model.svg": {"title": "OSI and four-layer DoD TCP/IP mapping", "description": "OSI application, presentation and session map broadly to TCP/IP application; transport to transport; network to internet; data link and physical to the network/link layer."}, "unit3-project-gantt.svg": {"title": "Year 12 Pygame project: twelve workshop slots", "description": "One investigate, two design, six build/integrate, two test/acceptance and one evaluation/handover slot. Exact weeks and lessons are in the milestone CSV."}, "unit3-routed-path.svg": {"title": "Two Ethernet LANs joined by a router", "description": "Client and switch A connect to router interface A on LAN A; router interface B and switch B connect to server on LAN B. A switch forwards a local frame; routing replaces link addresses and decrements IPv4 TTL."}, "unit3-secure-capacity.svg": {"title": "Shared uplink capacity", "description": "Twenty media clients each require 8 Mbit/s: total demand is 160 Mbit/s. A shared 100 Mbit/s uplink provides at most 5 Mbit/s per client under ideal equal sharing, before protocol overhead. Increasing the local uplink to 1 Gbit/s may help, but an unchanged 100 Mbit/s internet edge can remain the bottleneck."}, "unit3-secure-topology.svg": {"title": "Segmented media-centre design", "description": "The internet connection leads to an edge router/firewall, then a managed Layer 2 switch over an 802.1Q trunk. Four VLANs separate media, staff, guest and server traffic. Each subnet has its own gateway interface at the routing boundary. Inter-VLAN and internet traffic are subject to firewall policy; a Layer 2 switch alone does not route between VLANs. The labelled VLANs, subnets and gateways are listed in the diagram. Diagram labels: A logical design: four VLANs, one routed policy boundary; Internet / access termination; Edge router + firewall; Routing, gateway interfaces, rules; Managed layer 2 switch; 802.1Q trunk; 10 Media; 192.168.50.0/26; Gateway .1; 20 Staff; 192.168.50.64/27; Gateway .65; 30 Devices; 192.168.50.96/28; Gateway .97; 40 Visitors; 192.168.50.112/28; Gateway .113; Representative end devices: Media PCs; Staff PCs; printer .98; guest wireless clients.; Logical links, not physical port counts. Concept symbols; final task uses Cisco conventions.."}, "unit3-subnet-bits.svg": {"title": "IPv4 /27 boundary in the final octet", "description": "For192.168.50.70/27, the first24 bits and first3 bits of the last octet identify the network. Address last octet01000110 AND mask11100000 gives network01000000 or64. Five host bits give32 addresses."}, "unit3-transport-headers.svg": {"title": "Simplified minimum TCP and UDP headers", "description": "Each header row represents 32 bits. TCP has five rows, twenty bytes; UDP has two rows, eight bytes. TCP options are omitted."}, "unit3-vlsm-plan.svg": {"title": "Variable-length subnet plan inside192.168.50.0/24", "description": "Media uses0..63, Staff64..95, Devices96..111, Visitors112..127;128..255 remains a contiguous /25 reserve. Subnets are aligned and non-overlapping."}, "unit4-er-ten-table.svg": {"title": "Ten-table workshop booking interpretation model", "description": "Venue has zero or many Room rows; every Room row belongs to exactly one Venue. Room has zero or many Session rows; every Session row belongs to exactly one Room. Workshop has zero or many Session rows; every Session row belongs to exactly one Workshop. Instructor has zero or many Session rows; every Session row belongs to exactly one Instructor. Session has zero or many Booking rows; every Booking row belongs to exactly one Session. Member has zero or many Booking rows; every Booking row belongs to exactly one Member. Session has zero or many SessionEquipment rows; every SessionEquipment row belongs to exactly one Session. Equipment has zero or many SessionEquipment rows; every SessionEquipment row belongs to exactly one Equipment. Booking has zero or one Attendance rows; every Attendance row belongs to exactly one Booking. Attendance uses a joint foreign key to Booking. Booking and SessionEquipment each use a composite primary key."}, "unit4-workshop-model.svg": {"title": "Workshop booking model", "description": "Instructor and Workshop each have a one-to-many relationship with Session. Member and Session each have a one-to-many relationship with Booking, resolving their many-to-many relationship. Booking uses the composite primary key MemberID plus SessionID, both also foreign keys. Attendance uses that same pair as its primary key and composite foreign key to Booking. A booking has zero or one attendance record; no record means attendance has not been recorded. Present is 0 or 1. Session also records its date and capacity. Diagram labels: Workshop bookings: keys and participation; Instructor; PK InstructorID; DisplayName; Workshop; PK WorkshopID; Title; Member; PK MemberID; DisplayName; Session; PK SessionID; FK WorkshopID, InstructorID; SessionDate, Capacity; Booking; PK/FK MemberID, SessionID; BookedOn; Attendance; PK/FK pair -> Booking pair; Present (0/1); Legend: two bars = exactly one; circle + crow’s foot = zero or many; circle + bar = zero or one.; Each child points to its required parent. Attendance uses a joint foreign key; no row means unrecorded.."}};
